\title{zk.golf}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-24}

\p{
    A competition, run by zkSecurity, to write the cheapest circuit satisfying a fixed formal
    interface, with correctness proved in Lean using [Clean](dhsorens-002Y). Each challenge is a
    directory of Lean files rather than a prose problem statement, and the interesting design
    decision is which files are trusted. The interface file owns the input and output types, the
    assumptions, and the spec. The challenge file — described in its own header as the trusted
    boundary — declares the circuit and the theorems with their bodies left as \code{sorry}, so
    the obligation is exactly to supply terms of those types. A configuration file names the
    declarations that must be present and the axioms they may depend on. The implementation is
    unconstrained.
}

\p{
    The SHA-256 instance makes the interface concrete. Input is a 256-byte buffer encoded one byte
    per field element, plus a length; output is the digest as eight 32-bit words. The assumptions
    are that every buffer entry is a byte and that the length is below the buffer size. The spec is
    an equation: the digest equals SHA-256 of the first #{n} bytes, where SHA-256 is defined in the
    challenge repository over the naturals, with padding and compression written out. It is stated
    as a \code{GeneralFormalCircuit} rather than a \code{FormalCircuit}, and the reason is precise
    — the honest prover is additionally required to zero the buffer beyond the message length,
    which is an obligation on the prover and not an assumption the verifier may make. Coupling the
    two statements, as the simpler structure does, would have put a padding condition into the
    soundness theorem.
}

\p{
    Five theorems are required, and the three beyond soundness and completeness are the ones that
    make the score mean anything. Cost is a pair — witness allocations and constraint rows — and
    the requirement is that the circuit's count equals a declared pair for \em{every} input and at
    every offset, so the number is a property of the circuit rather than the outcome of a benchmark.
    Because it is a theorem, a submitted score is certified rather than measured.
}

\p{
    The second, \code{isR1CS}, fixes the shape of the constraint system. A single row is an asserted
    expression in which, along the addition spine, every summand is affine and at most one is a
    product of two affine forms — the shape #{A \cdot B - C}, one rank-one constraint
    #{\langle A, z \rangle \cdot \langle B, z \rangle = \langle C, z \rangle}. Degree three and
    above is rejected, and so is a sum of two products, which genuinely needs an auxiliary witness
    and a second row. The check is documented as a sound over-approximation: it counts products
    syntactically, so it can over-reject — #{ab + ac} is rank one but counts as two — and never
    under-counts, which is the direction that matters, because a true multi-row assertion is never
    certified as one row. Lookups and channel interactions are forbidden outright.
}

\p{
    That prohibition is what shapes every number in the competition. With no lookups, bitwise
    operations are bit-blasted: in the published SHA-256 solution a 32-bit XOR costs 32 allocations
    and 32 constraints, a 32-bit addition 33 and 34, and the majority function 64 of each. One round
    is two #{\Sigma} functions, a choice, a majority, and seven additions — 455 allocations and
    462 constraints. Sixty-four rounds, the message schedule, and the feed-forward addition make one
    block cost 40,280 and 40,880; five blocks with the padding checks and digest selection give the
    interface total, 204,224 allocations and 209,586 constraints. A single 256-byte SHA-256
    therefore scores 413,810, on the convention that the score is the sum of the two — and the
    same accounting puts a Keccak permutation at 153,600 of each, a 4096-bit RSA-with-SHA-256
    signature check at 412,130 and 415,006, and a secp256k1 scalar multiplication above eight
    million of each.
}

\p{
    The third theorem, \code{computableWitness}, requires that every witness generator depend only
    on the circuit's input and on cells already allocated. This is the obligation that keeps the
    other four honest. Nothing in soundness or completeness prevents a generator from reading a cell
    it has not yet assigned, or from being defined by a non-executable choice; a circuit with such a
    generator can be correct on both counts and still be one no prover can run. Requiring the
    property as a theorem is how the interface says \em{runnable} without saying anything about the
    implementation.
}

\p{
    Validation is then mechanical, and deliberately so. The submission must typecheck against the
    declared types, and its axiom dependencies must stay inside a whitelist — propositional
    extensionality, quotient soundness, classical choice, and, per instance, the assertion that the
    target prime is prime, which is asserted rather than proved because checking it in the kernel is
    expensive. There is a flag for re-checking with an independent kernel implementation, currently
    off. Nothing in the pipeline reads the circuit. zkSecurity's own framing of why this matters is
    that frontier models can now produce deeply optimised circuits with proofs in days; I cannot
    check that claim from the repository, but it is not the load-bearing one. The structural point
    is that the interface makes the question of who wrote the circuit irrelevant to whether it can
    be accepted, and a competition is a clean way to demonstrate it.
}

\p{
    Sources: the [zk-golf-challenges](https://github.com/zksecurity/zk-golf-challenges) repository,
    read at the initial public-challenge commit — \code{Challenge/Instances/SHA256} for the
    interface and the trusted boundary, \code{Challenge/Utils/CostR1CS.lean} for the cost and
    rank-one certification, \code{configs/} for the axiom whitelists, and \code{Solution/} for the
    published reference circuits and their costs; [zk.golf](https://zk.golf/) and
    [Fearless and Collaborative Optimization of Circuits](https://blog.zksecurity.xyz/posts/zkgolf/)
    for the competition's own account of scoring, which I have not read directly.
}
